# =============================================================================
# .gitignore
#
# READ THIS FIRST. Two rules below are global deny-by-default rules, and they
# will hide files you can plainly see in your working copy:
#
#   *.md    Every markdown file is ignored except the root README.md and a few
#           named exceptions. This is deliberate. The planning notes, agent
#           specifications, design contracts and evidence trees this project
#           was built from are WORKING MATERIAL for the machine it is developed
#           on - they are half-finished, they cite local paths, and their own
#           authors record that any count written into one goes stale within a
#           couple of commits. What the repository SHIPS is: the code, the
#           tests, README.md, and the four built-in skill packs the product
#           loads at runtime. Section "DOCUMENTS" has the rule and every
#           exception to it.
#
#   *.png   Every PNG is ignored except two named trees, because nearly every
#           PNG here is a screenshot a test run wrote and can write again.
#           Section "IMAGES" has the rule and both rescues.
#
# ADDING SOMETHING THAT MUST SHIP. Git applies the LAST matching rule, so a
# "!" exception works only if it sits AFTER the rule it overrides. If you add
# a document or an image that belongs in the repository:
#
#   1. add a "!" line beside the existing exceptions in the matching section
#      below - NOT at the top of this file, where it would be overridden;
#   2. say in a comment why that file has to ship;
#   3. confirm it with `git check-ignore -v <path>`, which prints nothing and
#      exits 1 once the path is no longer ignored, and then `git add` it.
#
# Nothing else will pick it up. `git add` on an ignored path is silent and
# `git add -A` skips it without a word, which is how a file that was supposed
# to ship goes missing for a month.
#
# Two conventions used throughout. A rule keeps the reason it exists next to
# it, and a rule that was REMOVED leaves its reasoning behind so it is not
# reinvented. Any document named in these comments that you cannot find in the
# repository is local-only by the *.md rule - the comment states the reasoning
# itself, so you never need to open one.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECRETS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `.env` matches that exact name only, so the three rules below would let a
# *backup* of it through: `.env.bak`, `.env.ci-bak`, `.env.2026-08-30` are all
# unmatched, all full of live keys, and all swept up by a single `git add -A`.
# That is not hypothetical - a tooling step moved `.env` aside mid-session and
# left exactly such a file sitting untracked in the repo root.
#
# `.env.*` closes it, and `!.env.example` keeps the one file that must ship.
# Add a further negation if another `.env.<something>` ever needs committing;
# do not widen the rule.
#
# `.env.local` and `.env.*.local` are already covered by `.env.*`. They are
# kept because they are the conventional spelling a contributor will look for,
# and a redundant deny rule costs nothing.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.env.local
.env.*.local


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PYTHON ENVIRONMENTS AND BYTECODE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WARNING about `lib/` and `env/` here, and `db/` further down: none of the
# three contains a slash, so each matches a directory of that name AT ANY
# DEPTH, not only at the repository root. They are written for virtualenv
# layouts, but `lib/` would also swallow a `frontend/src/lib/` if anyone
# created one, silently and with no error. Nothing in the tree is affected
# today. Anchor the rule (`/lib/`) before adding a source directory by any of
# those names.
.venv/
venv/
env/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
lib/
*.egg-info/
.eggs/

# macOS Finder metadata. It appears anywhere and belongs nowhere.
.DS_Store


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TEST, LINT AND TYPE-CHECK CACHES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# pyproject.toml configures pytest, and a contributor may reasonably run ruff
# or mypy even though this repo pins neither.
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.tox/


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COVERAGE OUTPUT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov/


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NODE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The frontend lives in frontend/, but this catches anyone who runs npm from
# the repository root instead.
node_modules/


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EDITOR AND IDE DIRECTORIES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `.vscode/*` contains a slash, so it is anchored to the repository ROOT and
# governs a root `.vscode/` only. The "!" line reserves the one file in there
# worth sharing - the extension recommendations - for whenever a root
# `.vscode/` appears. The committed one today is
# `frontend/.vscode/extensions.json`, which sits below the root and is
# therefore matched by neither line.
.idea/
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
*.swp


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RUN ARTIFACTS - regenerated by every run, never committed
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `.playwright-mcp/` is written by the Playwright MCP browser tools (page
# snapshots, console dumps, screenshots) wherever they are invoked from.
#
# `*.png` is one of the two global deny-by-default rules this file opens with,
# and it lives here, among the run artifacts, because that is what a PNG in
# this repository almost always is. The exceptions rescuing the two kinds that
# are NOT disposable are in the IMAGES section below; they have to come after
# this line to take effect, which is why they are not beside it.
.playwright-mcp/
output/
*.png
*.log


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOCAL DATABASES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The service defaults to output/validator-studio.db, which the `output/` rule
# above already covers; these catch a DATABASE_URL pointed somewhere else.
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CREWAI LOCAL STATE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# tokens.enc is the CrewAI AMP tracing credential: it is a secret, it expires,
# and it must never reach an image or a repository.
.checkpoints/
tokens.enc
db/


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DELIBERATELY COMMITTED - listed here only so the decisions stay visible
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There are no rules in this section. It exists because "why is this file in
# the repository?" is the same question as "why is this file absent from
# .gitignore?", and the answer belongs in one place.
#
#   uv.lock                  Committed, at the repository ROOT, because the
#                            host auto-detects it there to pin the build.
#                            Ignoring or moving it changes what production
#                            installs.
#
#   .agents/skills/          Vendored third-party CrewAI agent skills,
#   .claude/skills/          installed with `npx skills add crewaiinc/skills`.
#                            They were committed deliberately, to make the
#                            repository self-describing to any coding agent
#                            that opens it (many editors read .agents/skills;
#                            Claude Code reads .claude/skills).
#
#                            MEASURED 2026-09-06 and deliberately left alone:
#                            they are NOT in the index any more. Every file in
#                            both trees is a SKILL.md, and the global `*.md`
#                            rule below now swallows all of them -
#                            `git ls-files .agents/skills` answers nothing.
#                            Whether that is the intended consequence of making
#                            the documents local-only or a side effect nobody
#                            chose is an open question, and not one an ignore
#                            rule should settle quietly. Re-install with the
#                            command above; add a "!" exception in the
#                            DOCUMENTS section if they should ship again.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IMAGES - the rescues from the global `*.png` rule above
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RETIRED EXCEPTION, kept so it is not reinvented. An exception here once
# rescued exactly one file: frontend/src/assets/hero.png, imported by
# frontend/src/components/HelloWorld.vue, which nothing imported. It was kept
# only because deleting that dead scaffold was still an open decision, and an
# ignore rule must not settle a decision like that silently. The decision was
# taken - the cluster was deleted along with the brand work that replaced the
# last thing it was kept for - so the exception went with the file it rescued
# rather than sitting here rescuing nothing.
#
# There is no frontend source PNG left. Add this back, with the file's name in
# a comment, the day there is one.

# VISUAL BASELINES, which are the opposite of a run artifact.
#
# A screenshot Playwright WROTE during a run is disposable; a screenshot it
# COMPARES against is the assertion itself, and one that is not committed cannot
# fail. Before 2026-09-04 the global `*.png` rule swallowed both, so
# `run-canvas.spec.ts` had three baselines that existed on one machine and
# nowhere else - which means a fresh clone generated them on its first run and
# passed, whatever the canvas looked like.
#
# The `-<project>-<platform>` suffix Playwright appends is why this is safe:
# a Linux run looks for `-linux` files, finds none and writes its own rather
# than diffing against a Windows render of the same page.
!frontend/e2e/visual/**/*.png

# RUN-SHELL EVIDENCE, which is neither a run artifact nor a baseline.
#
# The run shell's definition of done is binding only if a reader who never saw
# the build conversation can execute every check in it and reach the same
# verdict - and half of its rows are answered by a screenshot at a named path,
# read by a cold reader who is handed the PNGs and nothing else. A
# captured-and-then-ignored screenshot cannot answer anything: it exists on one
# machine, the verdict table cites a path that is empty on a fresh clone, and
# the criterion silently becomes an assertion by the builder, which is never
# evidence.
#
# Scoped to that one directory rather than to `docs/**`, because the global
# rule above is right about everything else: a plot or a stray capture written
# somewhere in `docs/` is still disposable.
#
# One asymmetry this leaves, which nobody ruled on: the PNG half of that
# evidence tree ships and its MARKDOWN half does not, because the `*.md` rule
# below has no matching exception for it.
!docs/run-shell/evidence/**/*.png


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PDFs
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lecture source material - tens of megabytes of binary, not application
# source. No PDF is committed.
*.pdf


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# USER SKILL CACHE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A user's own SKILL.md packs are materialised on disk here as a CACHE of rows
# held in the database; the database is the source of truth, this directory is
# not.
#
# This is a DIRECTORY exclusion, and git cannot re-include a file whose parent
# directory is excluded - so the `!data/skills/**/*.md` exception below does
# NOT reach anything under here, in whichever order the two lines are written.
# The committed half is `data/skills/builtin/`, this repository's own four
# packs, which that exception does rescue.
data/skills/users/


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DOCUMENTS - the global `*.md` rule, and every exception to it
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Since 2026-09-05 the internal documents are local-only, and this repository
# is public. Every markdown file is ignored except the exceptions immediately
# below, which are the complete list.
#
# WHY. The planning notes, agent specifications, design contracts, review
# records and evidence trees are working material for the machine this is
# developed on. They are written for the people and agents building it, they
# cite absolute local paths and commits that were later rewritten, and their
# own authors record that a figure written into one of them goes stale within a
# couple of commits. Publishing them would publish a running commentary rather
# than documentation. What a fresh clone gets instead is the part that can be
# checked: the code, the tests, README.md, and the four built-in skill packs
# the product loads at runtime.
#
# CONSEQUENCE worth knowing before you write a test: a test that reads a
# document must SKIP when the document is absent, never fail. On a fresh clone,
# in CI, or inside a container these files simply are not there, and a suite
# that fails over a missing document fails over nothing.
#
# THE EXCEPTIONS, and why each one exists:
#
#   !/README.md            The repository's front page. The leading "/" anchors
#                          it to the ROOT, so a `docs/README.md` or a
#                          `frontend/README.md` stays ignored - deliberately:
#                          there is one published entry point, not several.
#
#   !data/skills/**/*.md   The built-in packs under `data/skills/builtin/` are
#                          not documentation ABOUT the product, they are DATA
#                          the product loads at runtime. A clone without them
#                          runs with an empty skill library. (`data/skills/
#                          users/` above is excluded as a directory and is not
#                          reachable by this exception - see that section.)
#
#   docs/observability/**  Has its own block below, with its own reason.
*.md
!/README.md
!data/skills/**/*.md

# THE OBSERVABILITY TREE - committed, alone among this repository's documents.
#
# The brief for that work required its definition of done, its audit and every
# artifact the definition of done names to be COMMITTED rather than local-only,
# for one reason: a criterion settled by a file nobody else can see is an
# assertion by the builder, not evidence. It is the argument the run-shell
# evidence block above makes, applied to a whole tree instead of to PNGs.
#
# Nothing under this tree may contain a credential value; the workers who
# produced it were told so, and the tree is scanned for them.
#
# Only three of these six lines are load-bearing today - `*.md`, `*.png` and
# `*.log` are the extensions this file ignores globally. `*.jpg`, `*.json` and
# `*.txt` match no deny rule, so those three negations currently do nothing.
# They are kept as a complete set so that adding a global rule for any of them
# later cannot silently drop this tree out of the index.
!docs/observability/**/*.md
!docs/observability/**/*.png
!docs/observability/**/*.jpg
!docs/observability/**/*.json
!docs/observability/**/*.txt
!docs/observability/**/*.log


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOCAL WORKSPACES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LinkedIn content workspace (2026-09-06): captures, script, Remotion project, carousel. Never committed.
/linkedin/
